---

<BaseLayout {title} {description} {canonical} {schema} lang="en-US">
  <div class="container">
    <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span class="sep" aria-hidden="true">/</span><a href="/finance">Finance</a><span class="sep" aria-hidden="true">/</span><span aria-current="page">What Is Inflation?</span></nav>

    <div class="page-head">
      <h1>What Is Inflation and How Does It Affect My Money?</h1>
      <p class="subtitle">The quiet force that makes your money worth less each year — and how to measure it.</p>
    </div>

    <section class="content-section">
      <p>You've noticed it at the grocery store and the gas pump: the same amount of money buys less than it used to. That's <strong>inflation</strong> — the gradual rise in the general level of prices, and the corresponding decline in the purchasing power of your money.</p>

      <h2>What inflation is</h2>
      <p>Inflation is usually expressed as an annual percentage. If inflation is <strong>3%</strong>, an item that costs $100 today will cost about <strong>$103</strong> a year from now. That 3% may sound small, but it compounds — which is where the real impact lives.</p>

      <h2>Why it matters more than it seems</h2>
      <p>Because inflation compounds, small rates add up over time. At 3% inflation, prices roughly <strong>double every 24 years</strong>. At 4%, they double in about 18 years. This is why the cost of everyday goods feels dramatically higher than it did a generation ago.</p>
      <p>Here's what $100 of purchasing power looks like over time at different inflation rates:</p>
      <table class="ref-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Inflation rate</th><th>$100 today in 10 years</th><th>In 20 years</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>2%</td><td>~$82</td><td>~$67</td></tr>
          <tr><td>3%</td><td>~$74</td><td>~$55</td></tr>
          <tr><td>5%</td><td>~$61</td><td>~$38</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>What it means for your savings</h2>
      <p>If your savings earn <strong>less than the inflation rate</strong>, you're losing purchasing power even though the dollar amount stays the same. A savings account paying 1% while inflation runs at 3% means your money is effectively shrinking by 2% a year in real terms.</p>
      <p>This is why financial advisors caution against keeping too much money in cash over the long term — cash is safe in nominal terms, but it slowly loses value in real terms.</p>

      <h2>How to protect your money</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Invest for the long term</strong> — assets like stocks and real estate have historically outpaced inflation over long periods.</li>
        <li><strong>Consider inflation-protected investments</strong> — such as Treasury Inflation-Protected Securities (TIPS) or Series I savings bonds.</li>
        <li><strong>Keep an emergency fund, not a fortune, in cash</strong> — cash has a job (immediate access), but excess cash above your emergency needs is exposed to erosion.</li>
      </ol>

      <p>To see exactly how inflation erodes a specific amount over time — or how much a past price would be worth today — use the inflation calculator below.</p>
      <p><a href="/finance/inflation-calculator">Calculate the impact of inflation →</a></p>

      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <h3>What causes inflation?</h3>
      <p>Inflation is usually driven by rising demand, rising production costs, or an increase in the money supply. Most periods of inflation involve a combination of these forces.</p>
      <h3>Is some inflation normal?</h3>
      <p>Yes. Central banks like the Federal Reserve generally target a low, stable inflation rate (around 2%), which supports a healthy economy. The problems arise when inflation is too high or unpredictable.</p>
      <h3>Does inflation affect debt?</h3>
      <p>Interestingly, moderate inflation can benefit borrowers: you repay fixed-rate debt with money that's worth less than when you borrowed it. It hurts savers and people on fixed incomes the most.</p>

      <p class="privacy-note">For education only, not financial advice.</p>
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
